  - <u>Hyperkalaemia</u>:

## Evaluation of the adult patient with hypokalaemia

- **Approach to hypokalaemia:**
  - <u>Hx</u> - cause of hypokalaemia is evident from history (e.g. diarrhoea, vomiting, drugs), especially if a detailed drug Hx is obtained
  - <u>Laboratory evaluation</u> - if the cause is uncertain, two major components to diagnostic evaluation:
    - **Assessment of urinary K excretion** - differentiation between renal K loss from other causes of hypoK (e.g. GI loss, insufficient intake, transcellular shift) by demonstrating an <u>inappropriate urinary excretion in facing hypoK</u>
    - **Assessment of acid-base status** - different causes of hypoK may be a/w metabolic acidosis or metabolic alkalosis
- **Assessment of urinary K excretion:**
  - 24h urine K - most accurate method of measuring urinary K excretion but impractical
  - Spot urine K concentration - extremely limited on its own and requires accounting for urine folume and osmolality
  - Urine K-to-creatine ratio - correlates well to 24h urine K excretion but may be limited in patients with low creatinine concentration
  - Transtubular K gradient - an index for demonstrating urinary K excretion
